  [1mThe rules[0m, as constants at the top of [38;5;153mattention.js[39m:

  - [1mOverdue[0m — [38;5;153mdueDate[39m is before today, task not done.
  - [1mDue soon[0m — [38;5;153mdueDate[39m is today or tomorrow (2-day window), task not done.
  - [1mStale[0m — no [38;5;153mdueDate[39m, not done, and [38;5;153mupdatedAt[39m older than [1m14 days[0m.

  Three decisions inside those rules worth calling out, because they're the difference between a useful nudge and one
  you learn to ignore:

  [1mStaleness only applies to tasks with no due date.[0m A task due three weeks out that you haven't touched isn't forgotten
  — it's scheduled. Flagging it is a false positive, and false positives are what kill this kind of feature. Due dates
  and staleness are meant to cover disjoint sets: things you planned, and things you didn't.

  [1mA task appears in at most one bucket[0m, precedence overdue > due soon > stale. Same task in two lists reads as two
  problems.

  [1mDone tasks never appear[0m, regardless of dates. An overdue-but-completed task is not a thing to be nudged about.

  [1mThe UI[0m — [38;5;153m<main>[39m gets two regions:

  1. [1m"Needs attention (N)"[0m at the top, rendered [3monly when non-empty[0m. Three labelled groups, most urgent first, each task
     showing why it's there ("3 days overdue", "untouched 18 days"). When nothing qualifies, the section is absent
     entirely — not "0 items need attention," which is a banner that teaches you to stop reading banners.
  2. [1mThe task list[0m below it: add form, title, due date, done checkbox, delete. Plain and unstyled beyond what's needed
     to make the attention section visually distinct.

  Attention is recomputed on load and after every mutation. No timers, no polling — with an in-app-only surface, the
  only moments that can matter are the moments you're looking at it.

  The 14-day and 2-day thresholds are guesses. They're single constants, trivially changed once you've used it; I'd
  resist building a settings UI for them until you know what's wrong with them.

  Look right?

